Excruciating monoarthritis (podagra), Exquisite joint tenderness/erythema, Tophi, Negatively birefringent urate crystals

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-014
Reason for Visit: Abrupt onset of excruciating, unmanageable pain in the right great toe preventing weight-bearing and sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute, severe, exquisitely painful monoarthritis rendering the patient completely immobile and in severe distress. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to exclude acute septic arthritis, which can destroy the joint within 24 hours, and to administer potent analgesia before morning clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up and my big toe feels like it's been smashed with a hammer and set on fire. I can't even let the bedsheet touch it."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an abrupt onset of excruciating joint pain in the right first metatarsophalangeal joint, commencing at 2 AM and peaking in intensity over the last 6 hours. The joint is exquisitely tender, swollen, erythematous, and warm, mimicking acute cellulitis. The pain is exacerbated by even the lightest touch. The patient reports a low-grade fever and localized chills. He attended a banquet last night involving heavy meat consumption and alcohol. He has a history of similar, self-resolving attacks years ago but never this severe.
Functional Status: Completely unable to weight-bear on the right foot; currently hopping or crawling to the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe pain causing extreme distress and sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.7 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98 percent on room air | Wt: 95 Kg
General: In overt, severe pain, guarding the right foot and visibly grimacing when the bed is moved.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals a classic podagra: the right first MTP joint is dramatically swollen, fiery red, hot to the touch, and exquisitely tender to light palpation. Range of motion is zero due to pain. Careful inspection of the pinnae of the ears reveals small, firm, chalky subcutaneous nodules consistent with chronic tophi. No skin breaks, lacerations, or tracking erythema beyond the joint capsule. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Excruciating monoarthritis (podagra), Exquisite joint tenderness/erythema, Tophi, and anticipated Negatively birefringent urate crystals upon joint aspiration if performed.
Environmental Observations: Heavy blankets removed from the bed to prevent contact with the affected joint.
Medication Reconciliation: Takes a regular thiazide diuretic for hypertension, a known precipitant for hyperuricaemia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Gouty Arthritis (Podagra) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Septic arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The classic first MTP location, the history of a dietary trigger (alcohol/meat), the presence of tophi, and the absence of a penetrating wound, though definitive exclusion requires joint aspiration if it fails to improve.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The erythema is strictly confined to the joint capsule without proximal streaking, and the pain is disproportionately severe for early cellulitis.

Pseudogout (CPPD) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The predilection for the first MTP joint (pseudogout favors larger joints like the knee) and the visible presence of urate tophi.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a classic, excruciating podagra attack causing complete functional immobility. Triage accurately recognized the severity of the pain and the potential differential of a joint-destroying septic arthritis. Objective examination confirmed the hallmark signs of an acute crystal-induced arthropathy triggered by diet and diuretics. Delaying assessment until standard business hours forced the patient to endure unmanageable pain and prolonged immobility. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to differentiate from joint infection, provide immediate high-dose pharmacological intervention, and restore basic mobility, fully satisfying the urgent MBS item criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral indomethacin 50mg immediately, to be followed by 25mg three times daily. Advised strictly resting the foot and applying ice packs wrapped in a towel for 20 minutes every 2 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly substituting alcohol and sugary sodas with 3 liters of plain water daily during an attack (action), you rapidly increase the kidneys' ability to flush the microscopic sharp crystals out of your blood (cause), which directly shortens the duration of this agonizing pain (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided a pair of crutches from the after-hours vehicle to allow safe transit to the bathroom without weight-bearing on the inflamed joint.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to assess resolution and discuss long-term urate-lowering therapy (e.g., allopurinol) and adjustment of the current thiazide diuretic.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness begins to spread rapidly up the foot and leg, if the fever spikes above 38.5 C, or if the patient becomes confused or intensely unwell, present to the nearest emergency department to rule out a severe joint infection.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a classic clinical presentation of gout, but if it does not begin to improve rapidly with the prescribed anti-inflammatory medication, hospital joint fluid testing is required.
Clinical Course: With high-dose anti-inflammatories, the agonizing pain usually significantly subsides within 24 to 48 hours. Without treatment, attacks can last weeks and permanently damage the joint cartilage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not stop taking the anti-inflammatory medication until the pain is completely gone for two days. Keep the foot elevated above the level of your heart whenever you are sitting or lying down.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Sharp, microscopic uric acid crystals have suddenly dumped into your toe joint, causing a massive, painful inflammatory response. The high-dose anti-inflammatory medication directly blocks this severe swelling and pain cascade.
Actionable Steps: Avoid all alcohol, red meat, and shellfish immediately. Drink at least 3 liters of water daily. Use the crutches provided to strictly avoid putting weight on the toe.

